// en-zen — one English assembled from the Englishes of East Asia.
//
// Built the way en-earth was: NOT an impersonation of any one accent, but the
// features on which Japanese, Korean, Cantonese, Mandarin and Tibetan speakers of
// English AGREE. Where they differ, the feature is left alone — an accent built
// from the intersection belongs to no one in particular, which is the point, and
// is why it can be a character rather than a caricature.
//
// The shared features, and the ones taken:
//   - SYLLABLE TIMING. Every one of these languages is syllable- or mora-timed,
//     English is stress-timed. This is the single largest difference and the one
//     that carries the calm: syllables of even length do not rush.
//   - Reduced vowel-LENGTH contrast — long and short vowels move toward each
//     other rather than one replacing the other.
//   - TH is not in any of these phoneme inventories. It goes to /s/ and /d/,
//     which is what the majority of speakers actually produce.
//   - Final stops are lightly released; the tap replaces the English approximant
//     /r/, which every one of these languages does something else with.
//
// What is NOT taken: the l/r merger, and vowel substitutions that change which
// WORD is heard. A voice that cannot be understood is not calm, it is obscure —
// and the whole purpose of this one is to be easy to listen to.

name en-zen
language en-zen 8
language en 22

phonemes en

// SYLLABLE-TIMED, AND SLOW. This is where the calm actually lives.
// The default English curve stretches stressed syllables hard and crushes
// unstressed ones; these numbers are far flatter, so no syllable is hurried past
// and none is leaned on. The whole set is also LONGER than default: unhurried is
// not the same as slow, but it is not achievable without some room.
stressLength 200 200 205 205 235 235 250 265

// Even amplitude too — emphasis exists, but it does not lunge.
stressAmp 16 16 17 17 19 19 21 22

// A pause between words. Syllable-timed speech has audible boundaries where
// stress-timed English runs words together, and that spacing is most of what
// makes this register sound considered rather than merely slow.
words 3

dictrules 8

// TH -> s and d. Not in any of the source inventories, and this is the
// substitution the majority produce.
replace 00 T s
replace 00 D d

// The English approximant /r/ becomes a tap. Safe: the tap exists in the table,
// so nothing is deleted.
replace 00 r *

// Vowel-length contrast reduced rather than removed: the long vowel moves toward
// the short one and keeps its identity. Deleting the distinction would change
// which word is heard, which this voice will not do.
replace 00 i: i
replace 00 u: u
